---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Overview
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

chatd is a single-threaded Internet chat server implementing a simple
application-level protocol over TCP. Clients connect, choose a screen name,
and may then broadcast messages to all users, send private messages, query
user statuses, and set their own status. The server supports up to 128
simultaneous clients.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Building
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

    make

Produces two binaries:
  chatd        - the server
  test_client  - scripted test client used by the test suite

Usage:

    ./chatd <port>

Run the test suite:

    make test

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Design
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Concurrency model:
  Single-threaded, using poll() to multiplex I/O across all file descriptors
  simultaneously (the listening socket plus one fd per connected client).
  poll() blocks until at least one fd is ready; the server then services each
  ready fd in turn before calling poll() again. No threads or mutexes are
  needed because only one thread ever touches shared state.

Protocol parsing — incremental state machine:
  TCP is a stream protocol; a single read() may return part of a message or
  multiple messages. Rather than blocking until a full message arrives (which
  would stall all other clients), each Client struct carries a small state
  machine with two states:

    ST_HEADER  Read one byte at a time, counting '|' characters, until three
               have been seen (version, code, and length fields). At that
               point parse the header, allocate a body buffer of the exact
               declared size, and transition to ST_BODY.

    ST_BODY    Bulk-read up to the remaining body bytes in one call. When the
               body is complete, call dispatch() and return to ST_HEADER.

  This design means the server never blocks mid-message waiting for more
  bytes; it simply returns to poll() and comes back when more data arrives.

Body length field:
  The spec requires reading exactly the declared number of body bytes and no
  more, to avoid consuming bytes that belong to the next message. We
  pre-allocate body_need+1 bytes and pass exactly (body_need - body_got) to
  read(), so we can never over-read.

Trailing '|' enforcement:
  After the body is fully received, dispatch() checks that the last byte is
  '|'. Failure is fatal (ERR 0 + close), as the framing is now unrecoverable.

Error handling:
  ERR 0 (Unreadable) — fatal; drop_client() is called.
  ERR 1–4            — recoverable; client stays connected and may retry.

Sender field:
  For MSG, the sender field supplied by the client is ignored. The server
  always substitutes the verified screen name from the client struct, so
  users cannot masquerade as each other.

Client departure:
  drop_client() closes the fd, frees the body buffer, clears the slot, and
  — if the client had a name — broadcasts a departure message to all
  remaining named users.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Test Plan
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

We tested using a combination of:
  1. Automated shell script (test_chatd.sh / "make test")
  2. Manual interactive testing with netcat

--- Automated tests (test_chatd.sh) ---

Test 1: Basic connection and NAM
  Connect a single client "Alice". Verify the server replies with a MSG
  containing "Welcome to the chat!".

Test 2: Duplicate screen name rejected
  Keep Alice connected. Connect a second client that also tries NAM "Alice".
  Verify the second client receives ERR code 1.

Test 3: SET status broadcast
  Connect Bob, send SET "Happy today". Verify all connected clients receive
  a MSG from #all containing "Bob is now".

Test 4: WHO single user with status
  Connect Alice, SET "I was here first". Connect Bob, send WHO "Alice".
  Verify Bob's reply from #all contains "I was here first".

Test 5: WHO unknown user
  Connect Carol, send WHO "nobody". Verify ERR code 2.

Test 6: WHO #all
  Connect Alice (with status) and Bob (no status). Bob sends WHO "#all".
  Verify the response lists both names.

Test 7: Illegal characters in name
  Attempt NAM "bad name!" (contains space and !). Verify ERR code 3.

Test 8: Name too long
  Attempt NAM with a 33-character name. Verify ERR code 4.

--- Manual / interactive tests (netcat) ---

Private messaging:
  Alice sends MSG to Bob (not #all). Verify only Bob receives it, Carol does not.

Broadcast messaging:
  Alice sends MSG to #all. Verify all named users receive it.

WHO with no status:
  User with no SET issues WHO about another user with no status. Verify
  response is "No status".

Sender masquerade:
  Client sends MSG with a forged sender field. Verify forwarded message
  shows the real sender name.

MSG before NAM:
  Client sends MSG without first sending NAM. Verify ERR Unreadable.

Malformed header (body not ending with '|'):
  Send a raw message missing the trailing pipe. Verify ERR 0 and close.

Unknown message code:
  Send code "FOO". Verify ERR 0 and close.

Concurrent I/O (poll test):
  Connect 10 clients simultaneously, all broadcasting at once. Verify no
  messages are lost or corrupted (checked by counting received lines).

Disconnect announcement:
  While two clients are connected, close one. Verify the remaining client
  receives the departure message.
